// @contract: C-030, C-212
// Cross-target differential fixture: `hex.encode` and `int.to_hex` in ONE program.
//
// The two live in DIFFERENT self-host sources (stdlib/hex_encode.almd and
// stdlib/int_hex.almd), and both used to define a `__`-private helper named
// `__hex_fill` — with different arities (5 params vs 4). The link-time dedup
// merged by NAME alone, so whichever linked first rebound the other module's
// call sites to a wrong signature: `--target wasm` rendered "successfully" and
// wasmtime rejected the module with `type mismatch: expected i64 but nothing on
// stack` — an invalid module escaping as `Ok`, the class the trust ledger audits
// as zero (#1068).
//
// It stayed at zero only because NO corpus program linked both modules. This
// fixture is that program. The class guard is the link-collision wall
// (`dedup_linked_by_name`, unit-tested in pipeline_link.rs); this is the
// instance guard — the two really do coexist, byte-identical on both targets.
import hex

fn main() -> Unit = {
  // int_hex's leg — expects: ff / 0 / 102
  println(int.to_hex(255))
  println(int.to_hex(0))
  println(int.to_hex(258))
  // hex_encode's leg, over the exact bytes from the issue's repro —
  // expects: 02010000 / 4869
  var b = bytes.new(0)
  bytes.append_u32_le(b, 258)
  println(hex.encode(b))
  println(hex.encode_upper(bytes.from_string("Hi")))
  // Both legs in ONE expression, so neither can be dead-code-eliminated away
  // from the other: whichever links first, the second must keep its own
  // helper — expects: fff:7a
  println(int.to_hex(4095) + ":" + hex.encode(bytes.from_string("z")))
}
